result.verdict:
From a docs-and-claims lens, PR #12 is close to mergeable. I rechecked every number I could against committed evidence and they hold: the decoder runs and medians (2.63x/3.35x) in research-two-symbol.json and research-word-refill.json, the 0.672227 payload ratio, the 24 KiB tables, the 26,519,936 + 16,793,600 = 43,313,536-byte arena, the replay/null table, and the 5.76%, 1.0576, 83.50%/99.55% and 9.65 GB/s arithmetic. I also ran the tests the notes cite and they pass: make test (GATE 3b shows 20 steps), the 93-layer row gate under ASan/UBSan and TSan, 9 routing and 3 lookahead tests, and trunk-rows CLI parity. Claims are consistently scoped as kernel-only or synthetic, with no full-model speedup. The PR adds no references to other repositories: only this repo, the upstream moonshotai/Kimi-K3 already on main, two arXiv papers and the io_uring man page. What needs fixing is mostly staleness that will ship to main at the final head:
- the CHANGELOG still says predictor work "pauses pending a real generation trajectory" after #13 closed that route;
- STATUS and research-queue still describe #12 as "under review" and "not merged";
- after #14's replay-free rollback, "--spec unchanged" and the "snapshot/replay integration" gate are out of date.
There is also one missing condition: all four "real K3 ranges" are slices of f_a_proj, about 0.12% of trunk bytes. Smaller issues: a CHANGELOG entry that still describes research-queue.md as the ranked per-machine list this PR removed, an unsourced 18.63 s/14.06 s split, the claim that the error-flag race was "uncovered by those gates", stale arena comments, and an --trunk-gb error message that does not match what the code enforces. A short docs pass, best done at the final head, should come before the stack merges. None of this blocks the code.

findings.detail:
The four pinned samples in research-two-symbol.json and research-word-refill.json are 1 MiB slices of language_model.model.layers.{1,12,24,36}.self_attn.f_a_proj.weight, the first four dense rows of lossless-samples.json. f_a_proj is 128x7168 BF16 per KDA layer: 69 x 1,835,008 B = 0.127 GB, about 0.12% of the 108.81 GB trunk. PR #12 presents these results as coming from "real K3 ranges", "the four sampled K3 ranges" or "pinned K3 ranges", without naming the family (research-results.md:70-72 and :86, CHANGELOG.md:21-23, research-queue.md:11):
- the 0.672227 retained payload;
- the 2.63x/3.35x median kernel ratios;
- the every-run 1 GB/s gate outcome.
Both compression and Huffman decode speed depend on the high-byte distribution. Later PRs in the stack treat the family as a required caveat ("on the four committed `f_a_proj` ranges only"; "gate 1 sampled two of 23 matrix families (4.00% of trunk bytes)"), but the Huffman section 2 remains unqualified at the final head (research-results.md:228 and :244).
